  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Isodalbergin may interact with the Sodium/Potassium Transporting ATPase subunits through a series of molecular pathways. The target of Isodalbergin includes both ERK and E-selectin, which in turn are targets of other compounds. These compounds, in turn, are linked to the Sodium/Potassium Transporting ATPase subunits, indicating a potential downstream effect of Isodalbergin on this cellular process. The scores from the paths suggest a moderate level of confidence in this relationship, with the strongest evidence coming from the direct targeting of the ATPase subunits by compounds that are themselves targets of Isodalbergin. The evidence is consistent across multiple paths, highlighting a possible mechanism involving the activation or modulation of signaling pathways that ultimately influence the ATPase activity. However, the exact nature of the interaction remains uncertain, and further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm the direct involvement of Isodalbergin in the regulation of these subunits.",
